00:00:00.399 --> 00:00:02.710 align:start position:0%
 
hi there steve kaufman  um i want to talk about vocabulary  because you know sometimes people say to  me well  uh you've heard of this uh elevator  statement you know if you had to  summarize in 20 seconds  what is link you know or  how does one learn languages  and it suddenly dawned on me that the  short statement of what language  learning is  is the following  learn the words and phrases of the  language  learn what they mean  and learn how they sound  that is in essence language learning  vocabulary  is by far in my view the most important  element of language learning  because  first of all as i pointed out earlier  while  a few thousand words may account for 80  percent of most  language contexts the remaining 20 which  has the key words to the meaning of the  passage  these  this remaining 20  requires a lot of words many of which  are relatively rare and  when we are in a situation where we  don't understand it's be very often  because we don't know  the words  now i posted a video in french  where i talked about  the subjunctive  and i said that i had heard that in the  canadian  public service  where people get a premium for speaking  french anglophones  francophones get a premium for speaking  english  but  anglophones get a premium for speaking  french and in their test to see if they  qualify for this premium they are tested  on the subjunctive  and in the subjunctive it's like in  english i wish i were is the subjunctive  form in english which we don't use very  much most people say i wish i was  but in french and other latin languages  or romance languages the subjunctive is  alive and well  but  learners especially anglophone learners  often don't quite pick up on the  subjunctive  uh in english if i say i wish i was  instead of i wish i were  everybody understands there is no  loss of understanding  uh through not using the subjunctive  so i said you know to fail people or to  deny them their bilingual premium  because they can't  use the subjunctive correctly to me is  just plain  silly in other words the subjunctive  really doesn't matter  if our main objective is communication  and i had a comment they were saying  well the subjunctive is important a  civil servant you know a public servant  should demonstrate you know a high level  of competence in the language  and i say  nonsense  the only thing that matters is the  ability to communicate and when it comes  to communication in the workplace  the number one thing is to understand  what's going on first and foremost  thereafter to get your meaning across  but we have had prime ministers in  canada  anglophones who spoke poor french  francophones who spoke poor english they  were still able to give speeches and get  their meaning across  if barack obama arrived in paris and sat  in on a meeting where everything took  place in french  and he participated and seemed to  understand exactly what people were  saying and even had some clever things  to say and he got his meaning across  but he didn't use the subjunctive  correctly nobody would mind it wouldn't  matter now if on the other hand someone  doesn't utter a statement without having  the subjunctive  right but doesn't have enough vocabulary  to understand what's going on or to  express themselves that can be a problem  so there is no question in my mind that  vocabulary trumps grammar  uh just as if trump's  accent or pronunciation  people might be jarred a bit if you  um speak english where you make the odd  grammatical mistake or if you have a bit  of an accent but if you have enough  words that a you understand what's being  said  and b you can get your meaning across  you'll make it and if you're a good  communicator in terms of you know all of  the other elements that go into  communication you'll do fine that's not  to say we shouldn't try  to uh to become  you know better in the language to  improve our pronunciation to master all  the fine points for sure but it's not so  important  unless of course you are you know  teaching the language or writing essays  in the language but for most people i  mean why do we learn languages we learn  languages in order to communicate and  that's why  when i speak chinese i try to get my  tones right my tones aren't too bad  actually they improved once i stopped  worrying about them  it's very difficult to get the gender  right all the time in french or german  it's very difficult they get the cases  right all the time in russian or german  but if we spend all our time worrying  about it we end up tongue-tied and we  can't speak so  my emphasis is always on vocabulary  learn the words and phrases of the  language  and if you learn the phrases you'll  start getting some of the grammar right  because it's part of these patterns  these phrases that you're picking up so  learn the words and phrases of the  language learn what they mean and learn  how they sound that's  my take on it and  by all means if one day like i would  love to speak russian without making any  mistakes on my cases but i'm very often  in discussions at link with our tutors  and i know far more words than some of  the other people who are participating  in the discussion who probably speak  more accurately than i do but i  understand exactly what the tutor is  saying in russian and i've got plenty of  words to stumble through my explanation  even with mistakes so  i say  hang the subjunctive  worry about it when you have time but in  the meantime focus in other words if the  uh you know the fairy godmother came up  to me and said you have a choice i can  give you  sufficient vocabulary so that you can  understand  everything you want to read and  everything you hear but you'll mistake  you'll make mistakes in the subjunctive  or i can enable you to be absolutely  correct in your use of the subjunctive  but you will have a very limited  vocabulary which would you prefer  no  question i would rather have the  vocabulary so there you have it thanks  for listening

